  These are candidate hotspots. A region used repeatedly by
  independent events is more interesting than a long tract used
  once, and it is the kind of claim a reviewer will accept from
  this dataset.

=== blocks relating to Node_147 ===
  on the branch INTO it   : 7 blocks  (Node_148->Node_147)
  on branches OUT of it   : 12 blocks  (Node_147->Node_114, Node_147->Node_146)

=== the ancestral event on the branch into Node_147 ===
  7 blocks, 3370 bp total
  branch                      start       end   length   SNPs   taxa
  Node_148->Node_147           3424      3492       69      5    100
  Node_148->Node_147          18491     18794      304      8    100
  Node_148->Node_147          21852     22722      871     11    100
  Node_148->Node_147          30395     31547     1153     13    100
  Node_148->Node_147          44604     44651       48      5    100
  Node_148->Node_147          83088     83396      309      9    100
  Node_148->Node_147         110014    110629      616     12    100

  All 7 blocks apply to the same 100 taxa, which is what a single event on one branch should look like.

  These junctions are the DIVA targets: carried by every
  descendant of the event, so an assay spanning one separates the
  recombinant lineage from the clonal backbone without depending
  on a tract confined to a sub-lineage.

  The 12 blocks on branches out of Node_147 are NOT
  suitable: each is confined to part of the clade. An earlier
  version of this script lumped them in with the ancestral
  blocks, which would have produced unusable assay targets.

